&lt;div class="metric-label"&gt;Live map&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The analysis page includes a county-level map built from official NCSBE election data.&lt;/p&gt;


&lt;span class="metric-value"&gt;24/7&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;div class="metric-label"&gt;Readable design&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Clean contrast, layered panels, and depth styling make the results easier to scan.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;Quick comparison&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Break down electoral patterns by county and contest so differences are easy to see at a glance.&lt;/p&gt;


&lt;h3&gt;Color-coded story&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Use a clear red-blue palette to communicate stronger support and relative vote share across regions.&lt;/p&gt;


&lt;h3&gt;Clean depth display&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Layered cards, soft shadows, and glassy surfaces add visual depth without losing readability.&lt;/p&gt;

Project purpose


This website demonstrates how a clean data story can be built around election results. The design emphasizes clarity, readability, and a polished presentation so that maps and summaries feel easy to interpret.




&lt;h3&gt;Data source&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Official North Carolina State Board of Elections results files are used for the county-level map in the analysis section.&lt;/p&gt;


&lt;h3&gt;Design goals&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The site uses layered panels, stronger color contrast, and a contrast-friendly layout to improve the visual depth of the experience.&lt;/p&gt;


&lt;h3&gt;Tools&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Quarto, R, and tidyverse are used to transform raw election data into a readable site with polished visuals.&lt;/p&gt;
